 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** The simulation resolution $dx, dy, dz$ must be much finer than the sub-volume dimensions to accurately model the phase accumulation. Typical values are $dx=dy=0.25\mu m, dz=0.5\mu m$ for $D_z=10\mu m$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The algorithm assumes a finite extent sample. Edge effects in the BPM propagation must be handled with appropriate padding or boundary conditions.
-*   **Lossless Assumption:** The equivalence between minimization and maximization (Eq. 5 to Eq. 6) holds only if the mode norms are conserved. If there is loss, the criterion must be adjusted [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Discrete Voxel Library:** The performance depends on the diversity and quality of the available voxel distributions $\{\Delta RI_d\}$. If the library is too coarse, the optimization may get stuck in local minima.
+*   **Precision:** The simulation resolution $dx, dy, dz$ must be much finer than the voxel dimensions to avoid numerical artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The algorithm assumes a finite extent sample. Boundary effects at $x, y$ edges may require padding or specific boundary conditions in the BPM [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Incoherence:** The modes are mutually incoherent, allowing the superposition principle to be applied independently for each mode in the overlap calculation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Losslessness:** The maximization criterion $O_{max}$ is derived from the minimization of $L_2$ distance under the assumption of lossless design (conservation of $||u_n||^2$ and $||v_n||^2$). If losses are present, the criterion may need adjustment [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The algorithm is iterative. Each iteration involves $K$ forward propagation steps and $K$ backward propagation steps. The voxel selection step for each sub-volume $v_{ijk}$ requires trying $D$ distributions. Thus, the complexity per iteration is $O(K \cdot I \cdot J \cdot D \cdot \text{BPM\_cost})$. BPM cost is typically $O(M \log M)$ for $M$ pixels.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the field distributions at each plane $z_k$ for all modes and sub-volumes, $O(K \cdot I \cdot J \cdot N \cdot M)$.
-*   **Strengths:** Allows for the design of complex 3D structures for multi-mode conversion. The iterative nature allows for local optimization, which can be more efficient than global optimization.
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive due to the large number of forward/backward propagations and voxel trials. Convergence is not guaranteed and depends on the initial guess and the voxel library.
-*   **When to use:** When designing CGVHs for mode sorting or conversion where high efficiency and specific mode mapping are required, and computational resources are available.
+*   **Complexity:** The algorithm is iterative. Each iteration involves $K$ forward and $K$ backward propagation steps. In each backward step, for each of the $I \times J$ sub-volumes, $D$ candidate distributions are tested. Each test involves a local propagation. The total complexity per iteration is roughly $O(K \cdot I \cdot J \cdot D \cdot \text{BPM\_cost})$. BPM cost is $O(M \log M)$ for a grid of size $M$.
+*   **Memory:** Requires storing fields at each plane $z_k$ for all $N$ modes, plus the voxel selection map. Memory scales with $N \cdot K \cdot \text{GridSize}$.
+*   **Strengths:** Provides an exact inverse design for mode conversion tasks. Handles multiple wavelengths and modes simultaneously.
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive due to the iterative nature and the need to test $D$ candidates for every voxel. Convergence is not guaranteed to be global optimum.
+*   **When to use:** When precise mode conversion is required for volumetric displays or optical communication systems, and computational resources allow for iterative optimization.
 
 ## Background
-The algorithm was proposed in the context of designing computer-generated volume holograms in glass for mode conversion [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the Iterative Propagation Algorithm (IPA) concept, where the global optimization problem is decomposed into local optimizations at each propagation plane. The method relies on the split-step Fourier Beam Propagation Method (BPM) for accurate wave propagation modeling.
+The method was introduced in the context of designing efficient computer-generated volume holograms in glass for mode sorting applications [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the Iterative Propagation Algorithm (IPA) concept, where overlap integrals are maximized iteratively [S1, unofficial]. The specific application to voxel-based CGVHs allows for the fabrication of complex 3D optical elements using femtosecond laser writing techniques, where discrete voxel distributions can be written into the glass [S1, unofficial].
 
